<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 93 0 R/Pg 10 0 R/A[299 0 R 0]/K[19]>>
endobj

122 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 94 0 R/Pg 11 0 R/A[300 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

123 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 94 0 R/T( SILENT NIGHT IN CAMEROON)/E( SILENT NIGHT IN CAMEROON)/Pg 11 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

124 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 94 0 R/T(MYSTERIOUS CLOUD LEAVES NO TRACE OF DESTRUCTION)/E(MYSTERIOUS CLOUD LEAVES NO TRACE OF DESTRUCTION)/Pg 11 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

125 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 94 0 R/E(CAMEROON HIGHLANDS \204 The tragedy at Lake Nyos will be remembered not only for its scale but for its silence. On the night of August 21, villages full of sound and activity became still within hours.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

126 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 94 0 R/E(Shortly after sunset, unusual noises were heard from the lake. Some described a low roar, others likened it to underground thunder. Almost immediately, a mist formed above the water. Witnesses said it spread outward in a thick sheet, moving downhill as if pulled by gravity.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

127 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 94 0 R/E(Unlike smoke that rises, the cloud stayed close to the ground. It rolled down into valleys, winding along roads and footpaths. Animals sensed it first. Cattle stumbled and fell, goats lay motionless, chickens went silent. Dogs no longer barked. Minutes later, villagers themselves collapsed in their homes and on paths.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

128 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 94 0 R/E(The following morning revealed a horrifying sight. Entire families remained in the positions they had been the night before: slumped near fires, lying in bed, or stretched along roads. Survivors who awoke hours later described confusion. \215Everything was quiet,\216 one said. \215The animals, the people\204gone. But the trees and houses were standing.\216)/Pg 11 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

129 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 94 0 R/E(Rescuers counted more than 1,700 people dead and thousands of animals lost. Yet the land showed no scars. Buildings stood unharmed. Crops remained green. Trees swayed in the breeze. The destruction was invisible, save for the silence it left behind.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

130 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 94 0 R/E(For the people of Cameroon, it was a night when life itself seemed stolen, leaving the landscape unchanged but the communities devastated.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

