147 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E( )/Pg 12 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

148 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E(NYOS VILLAGE \204 \215That night, I was sitting by the fire,\216 recalls one man who survived the events of August 21, 1986. \215I heard a noise, like thunder, but it came from the ground instead of the sky. My oil lamp flickered and went out, though it was still full of oil.\216)/Pg 12 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

149 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E(Confused, he stepped outside. \215The air felt heavy. My chest was tight, my body weak. I tried to call out, but my voice would not come. Then I collapsed.\216)/Pg 12 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

150 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E(When he awoke many hours later, the world around him was silent. \215I saw neighbors lying in doorways and on the ground. My cattle were gone, my family too. Everything was quiet. The houses stood. The crops were green. The sky was clear. But there was no life.\216)/Pg 12 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

151 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E(He remembers walking through the village, calling out in vain. \215Everyone was gone. It was as if the world had stopped. The land was the same, but the people were not there. The silence was everywhere.\216)/Pg 12 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

152 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E(His words capture the haunting reality of a night when the land remained intact, yet communities were erased in a matter of moments.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

153 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E<8D208E>/Pg 12 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

154 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E(\215I Woke to Silence\216: Survivor of Lake Nyos Speaks Out)/Pg 12 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

