<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(NEWS)/Pg 13 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

164 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(37 Found Dead After Rumblings at Crater Lake)/Pg 13 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

165 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(MONOUN, CAMEROON \204 In the early hours of August 15, 1984, something strange occurred at Lake Monoun, a crater lake in western Cameroon.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

166 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(Residents reported hearing a rumbling noise near the lake late that evening. By dawn, 37 bodies were discovered along a roadside east of the lake.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

167 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(The victims showed disturbing signs. Many had foam at their mouths, some had bloated stomachs, and others bore blisters on their skin. Alongside them were animals that had also perished: rats, snakes, even birds. The suddenness of the deaths startled survivors, who found their neighbors lifeless in positions suggesting they had been overcome almost instantly.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

168 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(Despite the heavy loss of life, the land appeared untouched. Crops grew tall in the fields. Trees stood unharmed. Houses were intact. Nothing in the environment seemed disturbed\204except for the silence of those who lived there.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

169 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(When investigators arrived, they too reported strange symptoms. Dizziness, nausea, and weakness set in as they examined the scene. Yet no sign of fire, flood, or landslide could be found. The mystery was left unsolved.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

170 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(The event at Lake Monoun foreshadowed the much larger tragedy that would later unfold at Lake Nyos.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

171 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 96 0 R/Pg 13 0 R/A[314 0 R 0]/K[13]>>
endobj

